China, Europe discuss AI technology, cross-border data flow amid trade tensions
BEIJING (Reuters) - China and the European Union on Monday discussed a range of topics, including artificial intelligence technology and cross-border data flows in Beijing, amid controversy over an EU probe on electric vehicle (EV) subsidies. Of China.
Beijing has criticized the probe as a "protectionist act" and warned that it would undermine economic ties.
China and the European Union said Monday's talks covered key issues, including fleets and data regulations, AI technology and cross-border industrial data flows.
The European Commission has expressed concern about "the difficulties faced by European companies in China in accessing their industry data due to recent enforcement."
The statement did not specify which laws Europe referred to. China expanded its anti-espionage law in July with extensive updates, including a ban on the transfer of any information related to national security and expanding the definition of the word espionage. According to Reuters.
"China welcomes companies from around the world, including Europe, to share their opportunities to develop their digital economy and achieve win-win results," Zhang told the meeting.
The two sides agreed to promote a fair and equitable open environment for the development of the digital economy and discussed other topics, including research and innovation in information and communication technology and security, Reuters reported. Of products sold online.
Global economic growth will slow to 2.7 percent by 2024 after being projected to grow 3 percent this year, Bloomberg reported, citing the latest OECD forecast. With the exception of 2020, when Kovid strikes the world, the projected growth for 2024 will be the lowest annual growth rate since the global financial crisis.
The Paris-based group has warned that risks to its outlook are skewed as past rate hikes could still be stronger than expected and inflation may continue to fall. Further monetary policy tightening. The group called the problems China is facing a "key risk" to economies around the world.
"After a stronger-than-expected 2023 start, driven by lower energy prices and China's reopening, global economic growth is expected to slow," the OECD said. "The impact of the tightening of monetary policy is becoming more apparent, businesses and consumer confidence are declining, and the economic recovery in China is slowing," it added.
