After officially ascending the throne, he made Koh Ker the capital, a major political center at the time. The other temples were built to house the main statues, which are large in size, aesthetically pleasing and lively. The statues include Isoram, Brahma, Narayan, as well as a group of statues depicting a scene of a battle between a group of monks and a group of monks of the Mahabharata.
After Jayavarman IV died in 941, his son Hassavarman II reigned for only a few years. In 944, King Rajendravarman II, a nephew of Jayavarman IV, ascended the throne and relocated the capital from Koh Ker to Angkor.
The capital of Koh Ker covers an area of about 4,000 hectares, which includes the city with the structure of roads, dry ponds, water reservoirs, especially the water system to suit the geographical location. In addition, in the capital, Koh Ker, there are also some important architectural structures such as palaces, temples for the king to worship, temples dedicated to the ancestors, temples for the public, worshipers, hospitals.
He built irrigation infrastructure such as dams, drains, ponds, and many temples in the new capital, such as Prasat Thom, a prominent architecture on Koh Ker. Not only in Koh Ker, there is also a place for the metal industry as well as the ceramics industry to serve and supply to local people as well as export.
In the Koh Ker temple resort area, there are 76 temples and 51 ancient sites. Koh Ker was still inhabited during the Angkorian period from the 9th to the 15th century, although it no longer served as the capital from 944.
The reason why this Koh Ker resort still plays an important role is probably from here, located along the ancient main road that connects Angkor to Wat Phu (now Laos) and to other locations in the Khmer Empire. That generation.
At the same time, the visit to Koh Ker temple resort refers to values such as the combination of geographical features and a new structure that clearly stands out as a city. On the other hand, the structure of the water system has continued to serve the use of the locals from ancient times to the present.
