Pursat Provincial Health Department Launches Campaign to Educate People on How to Prevent Dengue Fever
(Pursat): Under the leadership of Mr. Chheang Ra, Minister of Health and under the guidance of Mr. Khoy Rida, Governor of Pursat Provincial Board of Governors, Health Department of Pursat Provincial Administration has educated the people on how to prevent dengue fever. Chik will spray mosquito repellent in various communities in Pursat city.
The campaign will run for one week from September 19, 2023 onwards.
Khoy Di, director of the Pursat provincial administration of health, said that the promotion and spraying of mosquito repellent was to ensure the health of people without dengue fever, especially to give people a better understanding of how to protect themselves. Do not get dengue fever.
The director of the department said that with this campaign, he will continue to promote health centers, hospitals, referrals, community schools by spraying mosquito repellent and albatross to eliminate tiger mosquito breeding grounds to help prevent the spread of dengue fever. .
According to Khoy Di, tiger mosquitoes are a rapid transmission of dengue fever through water bodies (in car tires, motorcycle tires, cans, bottles, rainwater containers, water bottles). Therefore, please join in cleaning the forest around the house, keep the tires of the car and motorcycle that are covered in the jar, when the jars need to be covered regularly, because those places are the places where the tiger mosquitoes shelter after the rain.
Please be informed that the Ministry of Health has announced to the public that from January to September 12, 2023, there has been a significant increase in dengue cases. Experience, research and disease monitoring of the National Dengue Fever Program in the past shows that the dengue condition always has an abnormally high cycle of disease every 5 to 6 years.
Dengue fever can infect people of all ages, young and old, caused by the dengue virus, transmitted by the bite of a leopard-like mosquito that lays eggs in water-free environments. Smells such as: jars, basins, water containers, flower pots, bowls, table legs, cupboards, special cups, car tires, motorcycle tires, cans, glass bowls, etc., which are stored or disposed of in the open air, forming a source of water and mosquito nets.
Therefore, the Ministry of Health would like to remind all people to continue to be vigilant and responsible in protecting their personal and family health, to prevent the spread of dengue fever, and to participate in preventing large-scale dengue outbreaks. In the community to be more effective.
