Shock targets in the dolphin conservation area, if they do not cooperate in handing over the shock equipment, they will face court case
(Kratie): Kratie Fisheries Administration officials have warned fishermen in the Dolphin Conservation Area that they will have to submit their names to the provincial court for legal action if they refuse to hand over the equipment and stop their activities. No, that.
Mok Ponlok, director of the Kratie Fisheries Administration, said that cracking down on illegal fishing was a difficult task. The problem is the complex geography of the main river, especially the means of the shock group, the speed of the boat, making it difficult for the guards to chase them.
Based on the difficult reasons to crack down and give the perpetrators a chance to correct themselves, the Kratie Provincial Fisheries Administration District has chosen an education mechanism and persuaded the fishing industry to cooperate, hand over the electric equipment and stop their illegal activities.
As a result, out of 17 subjects, only 7 came to receive instructions and handed over 3 fish electric inverters (1 electric shock type and 2 electric shock type). Accessories).
For those who refuse to enter the two summonses, the competent authorities will issue a summons for the third time. If they refuse to re-enter, the fisheries officer will build a case and send it to the court to deal with the fisheries law.
Mr. Mok Ponlok: "In the future, if they continue to evade, refuse to enter, do not listen to the instructions, make a contract to hand over the evidence to us, we will ask the prosecutor based on the information of the relevant authorities to build a case to send to court." .
Residents applauded the actions of Kratie Fisheries Administration officials. They believe that efforts to stop fishing with electric shocks will make a significant contribution to reducing the threat to dolphin life and Mekong River fisheries resources. Minister of Inspection and Ministry Leaders Meet with 25 Capital-Provincial Departments
