Some members of the Aquaculture Association abandon their fish farming business due to loss, the Ministry calls for contacting professional officials through 1289 to get the right technique
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Fisheries Association says that about 60% of the farmers who are members of the Cambodian Fisheries Association, who are engaged in small-scale fish farming, have stopped raising fish, while medium- and large-scale farmers have reduced their fish farming. Due to the problem of rising fish feed prices, the fish on the market is cheaper.
About 60% of smallholder fish farmers have stopped raising fish during the last harvest in April 2023.
"During the fish harvest in April-May, smallholder farmers stopped raising about 60% of their fish due to rising fish feed," said Lem Phor Sothavarith, Chairman of the Executive Committee of the Cambodian Fisheries Association. "The price of fish on the market is lower, making farming not only unprofitable but also labor-intensive."
He said that medium and large-scale fish farmers have not stopped raising fish, but have reduced the amount of fish by about 40 percent, meaning that in the past, farmers used to raise 100,000 fish per pond, but now only fish. Only 6-70,000 juveniles because they do not see the clarity of the market price of fish.
Responding to the issue of reduction and cessation of fish farming by members of the Aquaculture Association, Ms. M. Rachana, spokesperson of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that due to the lack of fish farming techniques of the Aquaculture Association members, Raising fish is a problem until it is reduced and stopped.
Ms. Em Rachana said, "The decline in fish farming by about 60 percent is indeed a decline, but the decline in the production of members of the Cambodian Hydrological Association does not reflect the decline. "The whole of aquaculture in Cambodia, for family fish farmers or other farmers, still has a normal farming process."
The spokesperson added, "According to the aquaculture association's recognition and recognition, the members of the aquaculture association decided to abandon fish farming due to improper practices of fish farming, such as: "Overfishing and overfishing increase costs, reduce profits or reduce losses."
As for the communication of the people on Saturday and Sunday, the professional officer will reply back when he comes to work and if necessary, the Ministry will send a technical officer to help the people to this place, which is what the Ministry of Agriculture can Help you.
