Restrictions imposed by the European Union (EU) in May this year allowed Poland, Bulgaria, Hungary, Romania and Slovakia to ban the sale of Ukrainian agricultural products such as wheat, corn, rapeseed and sunflower seeds. Their countries, meanwhile, allow them to be transported across the five countries for export to markets elsewhere.
However, Poland, Slovakia and Hungary announced their own restrictions on imports of Ukrainian agricultural products last Friday after the European Commission decided not to renew the EU ban banning their imports. Holds the five EU countries next to Ukraine. The three Central European countries said they decided to do so for their own economic benefit and to protect the livelihoods of the three countries' farmers.
The European Union (EU) approved its ban last Friday after Ukraine claimed it would take steps to restrict agricultural exports to neighboring countries.
