The government data released on Sunday ahead of "Thanksgiving Day" on Monday also showed that the percentage of Japan's population aged 65 and over rose to 29.1 percent. In Italy, it came in second with 24.5 percent and Finland in third place with 23.6 percent. This means that Japan has the highest rate of aging population in the world.
For decades, Japan has seen its population shrink and age, while young people delay marriage and adoption, mainly due to job instability and hardship. Economics. In Japan, an estimated 12.59 million people are over the age of 80 and 20 million over the age of 75. Japan has a total population of 124.4 million.
As a result, Japan relies on an aging workforce. More than 9 million people in Japan are employed, accounting for 13.6 percent of Japan's total labor force, or one in seven workers in Japan. About a quarter of Japan's total elderly population is employed, down from 36.2 percent in South Korea but higher than other developed countries such as the United States at 18.6 percent and France at 3.9 percent. Percentage.
By 2040, the elderly population in Japan is projected to account for 34.8 percent of Japan's total population.
