1.5 million skills training program in the seventh mandate is a key strategy to strengthen the national economic force that Cambodians should be proud of
Prime Minister Hun Manet introduced the implementation of vocational training programs for approximately 1.5 million young people from poor and vulnerable families across the country and coordinated with stakeholders to allow workers Can attend professional training as well. In order to increase productivity and efficiency in the workplace, training courses must be organized at night or on weekends for workers.
This is a long-term consideration of the Royal Government in addition to building human resources, which is one of the five key priorities of the first phase of the Pentagon strategy: people, roads, water, electricity and technology.
This new program, in addition to teaching young people technical skills, can also increase the knowledge and skills of our workers, especially garment workers. That is, turning the use of pure labor into skilled workers, and workers who receive specific skills training through this special training will surely get better jobs and will definitely get more. More salary.
Simply put, if young people who have just graduated from high school or failed the exam and fall into a poor and vulnerable family, it is a priority to be able to enroll in the Technical Vocational Skills, a priority program of the Ministry of Labor. And vocational training that has already taught skills in manufacturing, construction, electricity, mechanics, automobiles, business services and cold and hot equipment.
In addition to these core skills, during the opening of the special training course, 1.5 million people will also be able to develop short-term skills in motorcycle repair, plumbing, air conditioning, salon decoration or food and beverage skills. .
Please note that there are 107 technical and vocational training institutions under the management of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training. There are 37 public vocational education and training institutions in 25 provincial capitals. The Ministry has also set up multi-professional vocational training sites in Phnom Penh, Siem Reap, Battambang, Kampong Thom and Banteay Meanchey.
The Ministry of Labor also announced that in Cambodia, our employment rate is about 99.3% and has a workforce of 10.8 million both inside and outside the country. Of that, 2.5 million were domestic workers, 3.4 million were agricultural workers, 3.1 million were service workers and 500,000 were public officials. There are about 1.3 million migrant or Cambodian migrant workers, while more than 830,000 are garment workers.
