Ministry of Agriculture: Cambodia is the country with the highest fish consumption, per capita consumption of 57 kg of fish per year
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Im Rachana, Undersecretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries on September 16, 2023, highlighted that in the world, Cambodia is a country with a high fish consumption. Most of all, a person eats about 57.3 kg of fish per year.
This emphasis was made on the occasion of Ms. Im Rachana's study visit and dissemination of post-harvest fisheries enterprises that benefited from the CAPFish project. The ceremony was held at Sen Pidor Hall of the Ministry of Agriculture on the morning of September 16, 2023.
Ms. Im Rachana stated, "Cambodia is one of the countries with the highest fish consumption in the world, with a population consuming about 57.3 kg of fish per year, according to figures from 2019 and "The highest in 2011 was 63.2 kg per year, compared to the world average of only 20.5 kg per person per year."
At the same time, Ms. Im Rachana said that fisheries and aquaculture resources are a source of nutrition, employment, and social, economic and cultural benefits. At the same time, more than 3 million Cambodians depend directly on fishery resources for their livelihood.
In addition, Ms. Im Rachana emphasized that the development of value chains focuses on technology, processing and quality management to ensure that Cambodian fishery products are both quality and safety to supply the domestic and foreign markets. .
At the same time, Ms. Im Rachana also stated that food safety is a priority of the Royal Government, food safety is not only a concern for public health, but it plays an important role for international trade competition. President Hun Manet Presents a Message to the King on UNESCO
(Phnom Penh): The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Moha Bovathip Hun Manet, pays a courtesy call on His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, on the inscription of the Koh Ker Temple on the World Heritage List. UNESCO official at the 45th session of the World Heritage Committee in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia on September 17, 2023.
After officially ascending the throne, he made Koh Ker the capital, a major political center at the time. Other temples are built to house the main statues, which are large in size, beautiful and lively. These statues include Preah Isoram, Preah Brahma, Preah Narayan, as well as a group of statues depicting a battle scene between a group of monks and a group of monks, excerpted from the story of Mahabharata.
After the death of Jayavarman IV in 941, his son Hassavarman II reigned for only a few years. In 944, King Rajendravarman II, a nephew of Jayavarman IV, ascended the throne and moved the capital from Koh Ker to Angkor.
The capital of Koh Ker covers an area of about 4,000 hectares, which includes the city with the structure of roads, dry ponds, water reservoirs, especially the water system to suit the geographical location. Apart from this, in Koh Ker, there are also some important architectural structures such as palaces, royal temples for the king to worship, temples dedicated to the ancestors, temples for the public to worship, hospitals and so on.
Jayavarman IV built irrigation infrastructure such as dams, drafts, ponds, and many temples in the new capital, such as Prasat Thom, the dominant architecture on Koh Ker. Not only in Koh Ker, there is also a place for the steel industry as well as the ceramics industry for local consumption and supply, as well as for export.
In the Koh Ker temple resort area, there are 76 temples and 51 ancient sites. Koh Ker was still inhabited during the Angkorian period from the 9th to the 15th century, although it no longer served as the central capital from 944.
At the same time, the visit to Koh Ker temple resort is based on values such as the combination of geographical features and a new structure that clearly stands out as a city. On the other hand, the structure of the water system has continued to serve the use of the locals from ancient times to the present.
