UN Special Rapporteur Tom Andrews praised Washington for imposing sanctions on foreign commercial banks, investment and trade banks in Myanmar in June, but said it needed to be tightened. Andrew urged Washington to work with other countries to stem the flow of weapons to the military regime.
Andrew also said it was important for Washington to at least provide humanitarian assistance to victims of the military regime, both inside and outside Myanmar.
In August, the United States expanded its sanctions against Myanmar by including foreign companies or individuals assisting the military regime in purchasing jet fuel used to launch air strikes amid speculation. The military has killed more than 3,900 civilians since the 2021 coup.
