More than 2,000 Indigenous and Local Communities in Koh Kong Province Benefit from REDD + South Cardamom Range Project
(Phnom Penh): Through the REDD + South Cardamom Mountains project, the Wildlife Alliance, in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment, has helped more than 2,000 indigenous people and local communities in Koh Kong province to better their livelihoods. Sustainability of clean water, roads, toilets and even help to study at the university.
All of these benefits are designed to respond to the requests and inputs of rural villagers in Koh Kong province.
"Red Plus" refers to programs to reduce greenhouse gas emissions caused by deforestation and deforestation. The REDD + South Cardamom Range project is led by the Ministry of Environment and implemented by the Wildlife Alliance since 2015. The project generates revenue from the sale of carbon credits, a large portion of which is used for financial benefits to sustain local communities and conserve natural resources.
In the past, villagers had to walk more than five kilometers to get dirty water. But now there are 94 solar water wells that provide clean water to more than 1,800 families. Five roads, 5 km long and longer, have been built to make it easier to access markets from remote villages.
Mr. Sisitha, from Chi Phat commune, Koh Kong province, said: “Due to the REDD + project in the southern Cardamom Mountains, a committee has been set up and now we have access to clean water flowing into our homes, which is pumped out. Wells. The commission must ensure that water is used sparingly so that all households enjoy the same benefits. "From now on, we must continue to protect our Cardamom Mountains to preserve water resources."
Tith Sour, an indigenous scholarship student from the Chhay Areng area, said, “Getting a special education through a bachelor's degree from the REDD + project has helped me develop my personal skills for a better future. And long for me as well as for my family in the future.
