A report from the United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF) and the World Bank found that the 19-year-old Kovid crisis has reduced the number of efforts to eradicate extreme child poverty from previous estimates. Up to 30 million people. As a result, an estimated 333 million children are currently living in extreme poverty.
Extreme poverty refers to a severe shortage of basic human needs, including food, safe drinking water, sanitation, health care, asylum, education and information, and an income of less than $ 2.15 per day. . The report also reflects the obstacles in the ambitious goal of the United Nations, which seeks to eradicate the extreme poverty of children by 2030.
According to the report, 40 percent of children in sub-Saharan Africa live in extreme poverty, one of the highest rates in the world. The World Bank and UNICEF have called on all countries to prioritize efforts to address children's poverty and take steps to achieve that goal, including expanding child welfare programs.
