Italian media reported this morning that Italy will withdraw from the Belt and Road Initiative and instead seek a revival of a strategic partnership agreement with China aimed at strengthening economic cooperation. It was first signed in 2004.
Italy is the only G7 country to sign the Belt and Road Initiative, a global trade and infrastructure plan modeled on the ancient Silk Road that connected China to the West.
Italy will chair the G7 next year, and reforming its relations with Beijing will make its Western allies fear Chinese influence.
"The key point is how to ensure that the partnership benefits both parties, regardless of our decision on the Belt and Road Initiative," she said.
