He added that a national commission will be set up under the leadership of His Majesty King Hun Manet to implement education reform in order to ensure efficiency.
India: G20 statement avoids condemning Russia's war in Ukraine and calls for peace
New Delhi: The G20 adopted a joint statement on the opening day of the summit on Saturday, avoiding Russia's condemnation of the war in Ukraine, but called on all countries to refrain from using force to seize territory. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Saturday, September 9, 2023.
Prime Minister Narendra Modi announced the adoption on the first day of the G20 summit in front of all leaders, including US President Joe Biden.
"We call on all countries to uphold the principles of international law, including respect for territorial integrity and sovereignty, international humanitarian law and the multilateral system that safeguards peace and stability," the statement said. We ... welcome all construction-related initiatives that support peace, justice and longevity in Ukraine. "The use or threat of the use of nuclear weapons is unacceptable."
The G20 also called for the implementation of the Black Sea Strait export initiatives on grain, food products and agricultural fertilizers from both Ukraine and Russia.
The agreement came as a shock to the war in Ukraine, with the West urging strong condemnation of Russia in the leader's statement, while other countries demanded that it focus on Solving economic problems.
