Russian, North Korean leaders set to meet to discuss weapons (Video inside) (Moscow): US officials say Russia is seeking more weapons for its war in Ukraine. The North Korean delegation recently traveled to Russia by train to plan for Kim Jong Un's visit this September, officials said.
North Korean leader Kim Jong Un is reportedly scheduled to travel to Russia this September to meet with Russian President Vladimir Putin to discuss the possibility of supplying Russia with more weapons for construction. The war in Ukraine. According to the report, Kim Jong Un's visit, whose exact date is not yet known, will discuss other military cooperation issues.
News leaked this week to the world over the plans and plans of the Russian and North Korean leaders were leaked by a US newspaper, leaking secret information from US officials. But how that information was known and taken is not explained in detail.
US officials, who leaked secrets related to the North Korean leader's trip, said that on a rare trip out of the country, Kim Jong Un was expected to travel from Pyongyang by train to Russia's Vladivostok is on the Pacific coast and is where North Korean leaders are expected to meet with Putin.
The spokeswoman said the United States had asked North Korea to suspend its arms sales negotiations with Russia and to honor its public commitment that Pyongyang would not distribute or sell arms to Russia.
The United States, from the very beginning of the war, has tried hard to release its secret documents in an attempt to discourage China, North Korea and other countries from intervening. Russia, while the United States itself and its allies can use their hands to provide a series of weapons and aid to Ukraine to help Russia lose the war.
The latest release of information about a planned meeting between North Korean and Russian leaders through its secret documents is also a new warning attempt to prevent the same. US officials note that a White House warning of a planned North Korean artillery transfer had helped end previous cooperation between Pyongyang and Moscow.
Blocking Russian support from North Korea, Iran and China is at the heart of the Biden administration's strategy for helping Ukraine defend itself against Russia.
The United States first warned of cooperation between North Korea and Russia a year ago. Russia plans to buy artillery from North Korea for use in Ukraine, officials said, citing US intelligence.
In a subsequent revelation, White House spokesman Kirby said North Korea had shipped weapons to Russia via the Middle East and North Africa.
U.S. officials say the leak has prevented North Korea from carrying a small number of North Korean-made weapons to the front lines in Ukraine.
It remains to be seen whether leaks of leaked information about a planned meeting between the Russian and North Korean leaders by the United States will now benefit any party.
