The survey predicts that it will take China until the middle of the 2040s for the country's GDP to surpass that of the United States, and even then, China's economy will outpace the United States in the short term. "Before falling back." Prior to the outbreak, China was expected to overtake the United States as the world's largest economy by the beginning of the next decade.
China's economy grew 3 percent last year, its slowest pace in decades as measures to prevent epidemics and the real estate crisis hit the country. The reopening of the country has given hope that the economy will recover this year.
But the economic recovery has lost momentum as exports have fallen and the real estate slump has intensified. According to Bloomberg News.
China's largest private real estate developer has failed to pay a total of $ 22.5 million in bond interest, which expired on Aug. 6, raising fears over its liquidity situation. The developer will keep the market afloat throughout the 30-day grace period.
Although the amount is small, the failure to pay interest will undermine the fragile hopes in the financial markets that China's policy momentum is beginning to erode the struggling real estate market. And a broader, more stable economy.
Failure to repay the debt will also increase the risk of bankruptcy and there will be more demands from other bondholders to expedite payments.
