France returns to Cambodia a piece of lip art confiscated by French customs
(Phnom Penh): On September 4, 2023, Mr. Keth Sophan, Cambodian Ambassador to France, traveled to Lyon to receive a lip sculpture confiscated by the French customs.
The five-piece sandstone sculpture, built in the 12th or 13th century and weighing about 300 kilograms, may have originated from Preah Khan, Kampong Svay or Banteay Chhmar. The sculpture was stolen in the early 1990s and was stolen. Seized as part of an operation to inspect the location of an antique auction house in Lyon in February 2020.
On behalf of the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, Mr. Keth Sophan expressed his deep gratitude to all the relevant French authorities for their efforts to do a good job. He stressed that the handing over of this cultural heritage reflects the excellent cooperation between Cambodia and France.
Finally, the handing over of this sculpture, which represents a part of history and a fragment of the soul of the Khmer Empire, will also allow for the reunification of the national soul and the Khmer identity.
