Mr. Kay Savin, Deputy Governor of Banlung City in charge of public order, said that the authorities have worked hard every day to organize like rain, but the practice of some traders refused to follow the instructions of the authorities and the authorities. The city has seized goods to issue warnings and fines many times, but some of them still come to shop on the public streets without fear of the law.
The deputy mayor also said that the houses on either side of the entrance to Banlung Market were affluent and most of the government officials rented the road in front of the house, which is a public street food, for rent to traders. Affect the public interest.
On the other hand, Oknha Salang, the investor of Banlung Market, recently said that the vendors on the public streets are not in order every day, not the investment area of the market, and this market is organized by the authorities. Organizers have also made frequent appeals, but the practice does not seem to be effective, despite the introduction or fines of groups that sell illegally on public roads.
