Cambodia-EU trade agreement on economic development, while Europe is Cambodia's main market!
(Phnom Penh): Economic growth and political stability are the main factors leading to increased investment in domestic consumer goods and exports. No country thrives without investment and relations between countries outside.
In fact, Cambodia has enjoyed high economic growth due to investment and trade, and much of that has come from political stability in the past.
In the last five years, Cambodia's exports have grown steadily, accounting for 45% of the economy in 2015 and more than 70% of the national economy by 2022.
Most of Cambodia's main markets are the United States and the European Union, with Europe being Cambodia's main market, accounting for up to 25% of Cambodia's total exports.
But the EU-Cambodia trade agreement is a hotly debated topic among Cambodian politicians. Opposition politicians sometimes want Europe to stop providing tariff preferences for Cambodian goods.
What is the truth between this agreement? What has the Royal Government done to keep this agreement?
If we look at the EU trade policy, we want to help 48 poor countries, including Cambodia, develop their trade through an industrial development with guaranteed working conditions, workers' rights, freedoms and respect for the rule of law. Democracy. With these improvements, Europe agreed to buy goods from that country based on preferential tariffs.
Since Cambodia reached the agreement in 2001, Cambodia's exports have steadily increased as the country has improved its share of the EU's demand, although the EU has provided 80% of Cambodia's goods. In the European market, which has been implemented since 2020.
In fact, Cambodia's exports to Europe in 2022 amounted to 4 billion US dollars, up from 2021 million US dollars in 2021, and Cambodia exports the most European countries are Germany, Mexico and the Netherlands.
These exports directly employ about 800,000 workers and provide another 1.2 million indirect benefits through the Cambodia-Europe Trade Agreement (EBA). These are the benefits that the Royal Government pays high attention to, firstly, strengthening the competitiveness of the enterprise and secondly, supporting the working conditions of the workers under the guise of obtaining trade preferences. European Union.
The Cambodian government is currently implementing a policy to increase the minimum wage for workers to ensure decent living conditions. The Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training has announced that special attention must be paid to the fact that in 2022, the minimum wage of workers was 194 US dollars, which is more than in 2021, which was 192 US dollars per month.
In addition, according to the announcement of the Royal Government of Cambodia is scheduled to meet with at least 200,000 workers after the actual election, the Prime Minister led by His Excellency Dr. Hun Manet met with nearly 20,000 workers on August 29, 2023 And nearly 20,000 more on September 1, 2023.
The Ministry of Labor also provides higher wages to workers among factories that are closed and suspended for any reason. For all these reasons, the Cambodian government pays close attention to the workers, a sector that has directly benefited from past Cambodia-EU trade policy. The garment and footwear sector accounted for about 35% of the economy, valued at more than $ 12 billion in 2022, of which Europe bought about $ 4 billion from Cambodia.
On the other hand, seeking additional markets is a mechanism by the Royal Government to ensure adequate employment of workers and greater participation in national economic growth, including China, Japan and South Korea, which are in high demand. Textile section.
Currently, there are two main channels for exporting Cambodian goods to the EU: first, the Generalized System of Preferences for All But the 80% Arms (EBA), which came into force in 2020, and second, the Generalized System of Preferences provided under the NGO framework. World Trade Organization (WTO), of which Cambodia became a member in 2004. The European Union is Cambodia's fourth largest trading partner and Cambodia's second largest market.
