PARIS (Reuters) - France will ban girls from wearing the Abaya, a long-sleeved shirt worn by some Muslim women in public schools, France's education minister has said ahead of the start of the school year. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Monday, August 28, 2023.
France, which has strictly banned religious symbols in public schools since the 19th century, has eliminated traditional Catholic influence from public education and sought to update the guidelines in response to the Muslim minority. Is growing.
Large crosses, headscarves of Jews or Muslims are not allowed in French public schools.
The move comes after months of debate as right-wing and far-right groups push for a ban, while leftists argue it violates civil rights.
