Evacuation operations continue in flood-hit areas in Pakistan's Punjab province as more than 250,000 people have been evacuated.
ISLAMABAD: Rescuers continued to evacuate people from flood-hit areas in Pakistan's Punjab province until this weekend, with about 250,000 people displaced so far. Safer. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on August 27, 2023.
Pakistani authorities have been evacuating people in the province after neighboring India diverted water from its reservoir this monsoon season and expanded rescue operations as the Sutlej River began to rise and flood. Some districts are nearby.
"The police and the district administration are evacuating people to safety and all available resources are being used to protect the lives and property of the people," said a National Disaster Management Authority official.
At least 213 people have been killed in Pakistan in heavy rains since the start of the monsoon season in late June, according to the authorities. About 250,000 people were evacuated from the affected areas as authorities mobilized boats and hundreds of personnel for rescue efforts.
Last year, Pakistan was devastated by weeks of unprecedented flooding, with one-third of the country flooded and affecting 33 million people. Taken 1,739 lives. The floods also damaged Pakistan's $ 30 billion economy.
