Mr. Sopheak, from a pagoda student to a music expert, is studying for a master's degree at the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
(Phnom Penh): Because of his love for traditional Khmer musical instruments and his desire to promote Khmer cultural heritage, Suon Sopheak decided to pursue a master's degree in music. He is studying and researching his dissertation at the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
This former child who used to study at the pagoda has long loved Khmer traditional musical instruments. This led him to pursue this skill by studying at the Fine Arts High School, then at the Royal University of Fine Arts and becoming an expert in music.
Young people from Kandal province can play many kinds of traditional Khmer musical instruments by playing the flute, such as the flute and the zither. In an interview with Fresh News, Suon Sopheak performed a number of songs as people around him applauded, which was a post-lunch break.
