The following is a Royal Decree: Business Awareness: Experts encourage farmers to use agricultural insurance to avoid losses due to declining yields or climate change.
Cambodian farmers have always been affected, especially in the last two years, some major crops such as mango, cashew, etc. Yields have dropped significantly due to climate change, which has caused farmers to lose both costs and profits. .
Insurance experts in Cambodia have encouraged farmers who grow crops such as rice, cashews and mangoes to use agricultural insurance to cover harvest losses.
Agricultural insurance is an important part of the development of the agricultural sector in Cambodia, especially at a time when the world is concerned about the rise of climate change. The agricultural sector in Cambodia is very important to contribute to the development of the national economy, which accounts for more than 20% of Cambodia's GDP. But very few Cambodian farmers understand and use agricultural insurance.
Mr. Youk Chamroeunbath, CEO of Forte Insurance Company, said that the agricultural insurance service has helped many farmers who can still earn some income or get rid of their farms to have capital to continue. Planted next season, although weather conditions have reduced their yields.
He added that Forte is currently working to promote outreach to farmers in some villages. Forte has been trying to provide insurance in three provinces, including Battambang, Kampong Thom and Prey Veng, with two districts being selected in each province.
The second type of insurance is output insurance. For this type of insurance is to compensate for the decline in yield at harvest. If one hectare of land is harvested 3 tons, but if the yield falls below 3 tons due to weather or pests, the company will compensate for the lost yield.
According to Mr. Youk Chamroeunbith, currently, farmers who use agricultural insurance services, including about 80,000 hectares of rice, use insurance for rice cultivation in the rainy season. There are about 200,000 mango trees and about 6,000 hectares of cashew nuts.
