Myanmar plans to restrict rice exports to curb domestic prices (Video inside) (Myanmar) - Myanmar plans to temporarily restrict rice exports to curb growth Rising domestic food prices by joining India, the world's largest rice exporter, amid tightening global supply.
"We will temporarily restrict rice exports for about 45 days from the end of this month," a senior member of the Myanmar Rice Federation told Reuters. The official added that rising domestic food prices were prompting authorities to restrict exports.
Myanmar is the world's fifth-largest rice exporter, selling more than 2 million tonnes a year, according to USDA data.
Last month, India banned the export of non-basmati white rice, cutting supplies on the world market by about 10 million tonnes or 20%.
"Myanmar is not a major exporter in the rice market like India or Thailand, but tightening is taking place in Mumbai," said a Mumbai rice trader from a world-class rice trading company. "When supply is tight."
"This will send an upward signal to the market and raise concerns for buyers," he added.
Global rice prices sold by major exporters, including Thailand and Vietnam, have hit a 15-year high since India decided to cut supplies.
