SEOUL (Reuters) - North Korea on Thursday said a second attempt to launch a reconnaissance satellite into orbit failed again three months after the attempt. The first failed, with rockets and satellites crashing into the sea shortly after launch.
The United States and its allies, such as South Korea and Japan, have called North Korea's launch of a spy satellite a violation of a UN Security Council resolution banning any development of dual-use technologies. The dual-use standard, which can be used in both civilian and military applications, could be used for the benefit of North Korea's ballistic missile program.
During an emergency meeting of the UN Security Council, 13 of the 15 members of the UN Security Council, excluding China and Russia, condemned Pyongyang's second satellite launch in three months. Uses ballistic missile technology.
