(Phnom Penh): Thai authorities will facilitate the release of five Cambodians working illegally in Thailand to return to Cambodia next week after co-ordination by the Cambodian Ministry of Labor and the Cambodian Embassy.
It should be noted that Thai authorities on August 19, 2023 arrested 5 Cambodians who were doing business that was prohibited by Thai law. Immediately, the Cambodian Embassy in Thailand sent a subcommittee to work out a solution and find out the problem to find a solution.
"Under close coordination between the two neighboring countries, the authorities have allowed the five Cambodians to return to Cambodia on August 29, 2023 next week," said the embassy's deputy chief of staff.
At the same time, he called on Cambodians who want to work in the country to become legal workers through agencies, companies that are officially recognized by the Ministry of Labor, because working illegally is risky, dangerous and loses benefits, but the service. Legally, it does not expose Cambodians to occupational risks and benefits to partner countries.
He added that the Cambodian Embassy and the Cambodian Deputy Attorney General in Thailand have not been indifferent to the well-being of the people facing the problem.
In addition, the Cambodian Labor Attorney in Thailand appealed to people who wish to work abroad legally and safely, as well as receive other benefits in accordance with the law in the host country, so that our Cambodian citizens can register. Work through a private recruitment agency licensed by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training and the Committee for Training and Sending Workers Abroad.
(Phnom Penh): Oknha Te Tang Po, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Samdech Techo Association for the Development of Small and Medium Enterprises in Cambodia, on August 24, 2023, chaired a meeting of the Board of Directors of the Association to discuss the initiative to establish a committee. Skilled workers in charge of negotiation and communication to assist small and medium enterprises in Cambodia (SMEs).
The eight commissions that the meeting will decide to set up are: Training and Technology Commission, International Trade Negotiation and Trade Commission, Customs, Transport and Logistics Customs Commission, Food Sanitation and Experimental Commission, Administrative Coordination Committee And Public Relations, the Coordinating and Member Relations Committee, the Research and Legal Commission, and the Audit and Tax Commission.
The meeting was held at the meeting hall of the Samdech Techo Foundation Business Association for the Development of Small and Medium Enterprises in Cambodia, with the participation of businessmen from various sectors in Cambodia.
Tycoon Te Tang Po, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Samdech Techo Foundation Association, said that the eight commissions will work with the Royal Government to address the challenges of SMEs.
He encouraged and encouraged the Oknha Oknha, the Board of Directors and in charge of the above eight commissions, to be responsible for the tasks assigned and organized and to be actively involved in promoting the private sector as well as Promote negotiations and other relations for cooperation and friendship with government institutions and SMEs, both inside and outside the country.
In addition, in early September, he will lead members to meet with the new Minister of Industry, Science and Innovation to work together to find solutions to the challenges of SMEs that have been facing in the past.
Oknha Sin Chanthy, President of the Cambodian Logistics Association, said that many enterprises from other sectors, such as food, agro-industry, have many problems to solve.
He added that the mission of these commissions is necessary to help solve problems for business owners and provide knowledge, technical skills, technology and training on various businesses to solve market and production problems. Through this commission set up will find ways to help those business owners, but if the problem can not be solved will submit a proposal to the government to help solve.
