BEIJING: China has stopped importing all seafood from Japan, raising tensions between the two nations as Japan begins to drain treated wastewater from the Fukushima nuclear power plant. According to Bloomberg News.
The "extensive" suspension of Japanese fishery products went into effect on Thursday, Bloomberg reported, citing a statement issued by the Chinese Customs Office.
Japan has also stressed that similar effluents are common. A two-year assessment conducted by the International Atomic Energy Agency found that Japan's strategy would have minimal impact on humans and the environment.
However, China has accused the effluent of being "selfish and irresponsible." The Chinese customs authority said the suspension was aimed at preventing the risk of radiation exposure.
(Thailand) - A recent study by the Thai Ministry of Commerce shows that Thailand's tourism and exports of chemicals and plastics are expected to slow as the Chinese economy is slowing. According to the Bangkok Post.
Earlier this month, the People's Bank of China announced "Three Red Lines" criteria aimed at reducing the risk of excessive real estate prices and preventing rising debt in real estate developers. Only 6.3% of Chinese real estate companies meet these criteria, making it almost impossible for most companies to borrow more money to run their operations.
Coupled with declining sales during an epidemic, many businesses are facing serious financial difficulties, including large-scale companies Evergrande.
Due to the large size of the real estate sector, which accounts for about 29% of China's GDP, the sector's problems have serious consequences for the Chinese economy. In the second quarter of this year, the economy grew by only 0.8% compared to the first quarter.
This factor also affects the financial situation of the population, as about 70% of the urban population invests in real estate to earn a living, and falling property prices lead to significant financial losses.
According to Poonpong, the aftermath of these events also affected the Thai economy, especially tourism and consumer spending abroad, among Chinese and other impacts.
In the first half of 2023, only 1.4 million Chinese tourists visited Thailand, a significant decrease.
