ADB: Epidemic and inflation push 68 million more people in Asia into poverty
Manila: 19 Kovid epidemics and rising living costs pushed nearly 70 million more people into extreme poverty in developing Asia last year, undermining efforts to Eliminate poverty. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Thursday, August 24, 2023.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) said in a new report released on Thursday that 155.2 million people in Asia are developing, or 3.9 percent of the region's total population. Live in extreme poverty by 2022, an increase of 67.8 million people.
There are 46 developing countries in the Asia-Pacific region, excluding Japan, Australia and New Zealand.
Rising living costs are affecting everyone but the poorest, as they have to spend more on food and fuel, making it harder for them to save and pay for essentials such as: Health care and education.
